::GEO_U04_L04_GP_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L04_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.1
Question:
Two similar rectangles have corresponding side lengths 5 and 15. What is the scale factor from the smaller rectangle to the larger rectangle?
{
=A. 3#The scale factor is 15 divided by 5, which equals 3.
~B. ⅓#This is the reverse scale factor from larger to smaller.
~C. 10#This subtracts the side lengths instead of comparing them multiplicatively.
~D. 20#This adds the side lengths and does not describe scale factor.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_GP_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L04_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.1
Question:
A triangle has side 8 corresponding to side 12 in a similar triangle. If another side is 10, what is the corresponding side length in the larger triangle?
{
~A. 14#Adding 4 repeats the first side difference, but similar figures use multiplication by scale factor.
=B. 15#The scale factor is 12/8 \= 1.5, and 10(1.5) \= 15.
~C. 6#This uses the inverse relationship instead of scaling up.
~D. 18#This does not use the scale factor 1.5 from the corresponding sides.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_GP_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L04_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.1
Question:
Two congruent figures are also similar. What is their scale factor?
{
~A. 0#A scale factor of 0 would collapse lengths and cannot describe congruent figures.
~B. 2#A scale factor of 2 changes size, so the figures would not be congruent.
=C. 1#Congruent figures have equal corresponding lengths, so the scale factor is 1.
~D. ½#A scale factor of ½ changes size, so the figures would not be congruent.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_GP_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L04_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.1
Question:
A map scale says 1 inch represents 20 miles. If two towns are 3 inches apart on the map, how far apart are they in reality?
{
~A. 23 miles#Adding 3 and 20 does not use the scale relationship.
~B. 6.7 miles#Dividing 20 by 3 reverses the map-scale relationship.
~C. 40 miles#This would match 2 inches, not 3 inches.
=D. 60 miles#Use the scale proportion: 3 inches represents 3(20) \= 60 miles.
}

::GEO_U04_L04_GP_Q05::
Question ID: GEO_U04_L04_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.1
Question:
A 6-foot person casts a 9-foot shadow. A nearby flagpole casts a 27-foot shadow. Assuming similar triangles, how tall is the flagpole?
{
=A. 18 feet#The shadow scale factor is 27/9 \= 3, so the height is 6(3) \= 18 feet.
~B. 12 feet#This does not use the scale factor from the shadows.
~C. 30 feet#Adding 21 to the height copies a difference rather than using proportional reasoning.
~D. 40.5 feet#Multiplying 27 by 1.5 does not match the person-shadow ratio.
}

